			<div class="content" id="post-content-2358925">
									

<p>Thoughts on the new one!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>After last attempt, pros:<br />
- I like that you kept the live test.<br />
- I like the people in the log. I like their discussion, the previous anomaly, and all that.<br />
- I like the photograph as the source, and them trying to track down the body and camera. That's a neat, intimate detail, as you say, and it's narratively a lot more more interesting than trying to blast it into space.</p>
<p>Cons:<br />
- The picture's kinda cheesy.<br />
- Still chock full of technical jargon. The containment procedures, for instance, <em>sound</em> good, but I have no direct idea what they mean. I can get &quot;Put the bad stuff in special bins, etc&quot; from context, but it just seems to run long.<br />
- The log droned on and on. There were very interesting parts (the banter, the blood vessel comparison) but my eyes glazed over for a lot of it. It's realistic on the level of what a full, unedited log would sound like, but as a reader a lot of it is extraneous.</p>
<p>Bonus point: I might have had some benefit in understanding it from reading the previous version, so I would listen to others on clarity issues.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Ultimately, no vote. This is much closer to an article I could get behind, but it could use some careful editing. I'm not saying take a machete to it haphazardly, but you can take a step back and figure out what needs to be there to get info to your readers and what doesn't communicate or is too couched in jargon to help.</p>

							</div>

								
					
